1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:05,355
At the start of every academic year,

2
00:00:05,395 --> 00:00:09,815
schools and educational institutions
face the same questions.

3
00:00:10,655 --> 00:00:15,656
Which books will we teach? Which
edition? For which grades and tracks?

4
00:00:16,496 --> 00:00:21,924
Who follows up on homework, tests and
grades? And where are the answers kept?

5
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:28,746
In many institutions, those answers are
spread across separate spreadsheets,

6
00:00:28,786 --> 00:00:33,070
chat groups and tools that
do not connect to each other.

7
00:00:33,910 --> 00:00:36,467
Each department may work its own way,

8
00:00:36,507 --> 00:00:40,696
so a unified picture of what is
really happening is hard to get.

9
00:00:41,536 --> 00:00:45,454
Manual follow-up takes up
teachers’ and administrators’

10
00:00:45,494 --> 00:00:48,764
time, instead of it
going to teaching itself.

11
00:00:49,604 --> 00:00:53,594
And the student may have no
single place to see what is due,

12
00:00:53,634 --> 00:00:57,014
what they have done, and
how they are progressing.

13
00:00:57,854 --> 00:01:01,027
These are not verdicts
on every institution,

14
00:01:01,067 --> 00:01:05,461
but questions we hear often —
and they deserve a better answer.

15
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:10,862
So how can an educational
institution connect its people,

16
00:01:10,902 --> 00:01:15,627
its processes and the learning
experience in one integrated platform?

17
00:01:18,867 --> 00:01:22,400
Introducing: Curriculum
Scope — نطاق المناهج.

18
00:01:29,233 --> 00:01:34,181
The idea began with a simple
tool: a single page for choosing

19
00:01:34,221 --> 00:01:38,350
Egypt’s curriculum, from
Primary 1 to Secondary 3.

20
00:01:39,190 --> 00:01:44,643
It was designed to define the
content scope of an education

21
00:01:44,683 --> 00:01:48,530
product for the 2026/2027 academic year.

22
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:56,926
But it worked in one browser only — no
accounts, no sharing, no record of decisions.

23
00:01:57,766 --> 00:02:00,972
Today it is an integrated cloud platform,

24
00:02:01,012 --> 00:02:06,291
delivered as SaaS, for school teams,
their teachers and their students.

25
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:10,946
The platform rests on
three connected layers.

26
00:02:11,786 --> 00:02:15,821
The first is the curriculum: an
up-to-date catalogue of Egypt’s curricula,

27
00:02:15,861 --> 00:02:20,253
where the institution decides what it
covers and keeps a record of that decision.

28
00:02:21,093 --> 00:02:24,372
The second is the classroom: classes,

29
00:02:24,412 --> 00:02:29,853
homework, online tests and a
gradebook, built on that curriculum.

30
00:02:30,693 --> 00:02:33,498
The third is the institution: roles,

31
00:02:33,538 --> 00:02:37,767
permissions, subscriptions,
the audit log and protection.

32
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:42,771
The value of integration is
that information is entered

33
00:02:42,811 --> 00:02:45,476
once, then travels to where it is needed.

34
00:02:46,316 --> 00:02:50,144
The books the school chooses
become the class’s curriculum;

35
00:02:50,184 --> 00:02:56,867
from them the teacher picks the homework material, and
the mark then reaches the gradebook and the student.

36
00:02:57,707 --> 00:03:05,407
All with one account, one sign-in and
one interface, in Arabic and English.

37
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:15,233
And it is built on clear principles: Arabic
first, with full right-to-left support;

38
00:03:15,723 --> 00:03:19,922
privacy by design,
accessibility for everyone,

39
00:03:19,962 --> 00:03:24,894
and automated tests that
check every main user journey.

40
00:03:30,233 --> 00:03:33,345
Who is this platform for?
Groups with different

41
00:03:33,385 --> 00:03:36,200
needs, brought together by the curriculum.

42
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:41,588
First: private and international
schools, and school groups.

43
00:03:42,428 --> 00:03:46,507
They need to coordinate what is
taught in every grade and every

44
00:03:46,547 --> 00:03:50,566
campus, and to follow the daily
work of teachers and students.

45
00:03:51,406 --> 00:03:57,417
The platform gives them shared content
scopes, comparison between campuses,

46
00:03:57,457 --> 00:04:02,572
classes, homework and tests, a
gradebook, and a student portal.

47
00:04:03,412 --> 00:04:08,816
Second: education technology companies,
content producers and publishers.

48
00:04:09,656 --> 00:04:15,212
Their key question: which part of
the curriculum do we cover this year?

49
00:04:15,252 --> 00:04:22,553
The platform keeps the answer and sends it to
their systems through an API and signed webhooks.

50
00:04:23,393 --> 00:04:27,480
Third: tutoring centres
and curriculum consultants,

51
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:32,477
who prepare clear coverage plans,
export them and compare them.

52
00:04:33,317 --> 00:04:37,625
Universities and higher
education are a later expansion

53
00:04:37,665 --> 00:04:41,474
opportunity; they are not
fully supported today.

54
00:04:42,314 --> 00:04:49,860
Classes, tests and the gradebook can be used,
but the catalogue is designed for pre-university

55
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:55,474
education, and there are no credit
hours or course registration yet.

56
00:04:56,314 --> 00:04:59,839
In every case, the
intended value is the same:

57
00:04:59,879 --> 00:05:04,980
a clear, documented decision, less
repeated work, and a clearer view.

58
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:09,443
It is value that partner
institutions will measure

59
00:05:09,483 --> 00:05:12,910
in the pilot — not figures
we claim in advance.

60
00:05:18,167 --> 00:05:22,933
Let’s start the tour at the beginning.
Mona creates an account for her

61
00:05:22,973 --> 00:05:27,233
school in one step: her name,
her email and the school’s name.

62
00:05:28,073 --> 00:05:31,866
She receives an email
to confirm her address,

63
00:05:31,906 --> 00:05:37,503
then signs in, and her first
organization starts with a 14-day trial.

64
00:05:38,733 --> 00:05:43,043
To protect accounts, the platform
supports two-step verification:

65
00:05:43,083 --> 00:05:46,106
it is mandatory for
platform administrators,

66
00:05:46,146 --> 00:05:50,150
and an organization can require
it for its owner and admins.

67
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:55,637
From the account page, each user
turns on two-step verification,

68
00:05:55,677 --> 00:06:01,076
sees their active sessions, and downloads
their personal data whenever they like.

69
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:09,629
Next, Mona invites her team. Each member
has a role: owner, admin, editor or viewer.

70
00:06:10,469 --> 00:06:15,743
Karim receives the invitation link,
chooses his password and joins right away.

71
00:06:16,583 --> 00:06:19,571
Permissions are enforced
on the server itself,

72
00:06:19,611 --> 00:06:24,113
not only in the interface: a viewer
can see, but cannot change anything.

73
00:06:25,367 --> 00:06:29,504
One person can belong to more
than one organization and switch

74
00:06:29,544 --> 00:06:32,945
between them from the menu
at the top of the page.

75
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,621
This is multi-tenancy at work: every
organization is isolated from the

76
00:06:39,661 --> 00:06:45,133
others inside the database itself,
with row-level security policies.

77
00:06:46,567 --> 00:06:50,013
Navigation is clear: the curriculum guide,

78
00:06:50,053 --> 00:06:55,679
scopes, classes, students, members,
billing, settings and the audit log.

79
00:06:56,519 --> 00:07:01,075
The notifications bell tells each member
what concerns them, in their own language.

80
00:07:01,915 --> 00:07:05,850
With one click, the whole
interface switches from Arabic

81
00:07:05,890 --> 00:07:09,568
to English, and from
right-to-left to left-to-right.

82
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:16,650
The same interface works on a phone,
and it is designed to be accessible:

83
00:07:16,690 --> 00:07:23,435
149 screens were checked automatically
against the WCAG guidelines, with no findings.

84
00:07:24,275 --> 00:07:29,906
A manual review with a screen reader
is still a step ahead of the launch.

85
00:07:31,133 --> 00:07:36,456
Every important action is recorded in
the audit log: who did what, and when.

86
00:07:37,296 --> 00:07:42,303
At platform level, the operations
team has an admin dashboard showing

87
00:07:42,343 --> 00:07:46,408
organizations, subscriptions,
system health and alerts.

88
00:07:47,248 --> 00:07:52,257
But it cannot see a school’s work
unless the school grants temporary

89
00:07:52,297 --> 00:07:56,692
access, and every access is
logged in the school’s own log.

90
00:07:58,933 --> 00:08:03,516
Now to the features in detail, as
they actually work in the platform.

91
00:08:04,567 --> 00:08:09,190
At the heart of the platform
is the curriculum catalogue:

92
00:08:09,230 --> 00:08:16,444
twelve grades, five tracks and 231 subjects,
with more than a thousand books and references.

93
00:08:17,284 --> 00:08:21,223
From Ministry schools to
the new Egyptian Bakaloria,

94
00:08:21,263 --> 00:08:24,653
Thanaweya Amma and
international programmes.

95
00:08:25,493 --> 00:08:28,902
The team picks grades, then
ticks books with a click,

96
00:08:28,942 --> 00:08:31,590
and every choice is saved automatically.

97
00:08:32,733 --> 00:08:37,107
The platform team records the
review of each subject and its

98
00:08:37,147 --> 00:08:41,120
source, and schools see a
verification mark beside it.

99
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:45,444
So everyone knows what has been
checked, and against which source.

100
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:50,028
Each selection is saved
as a named content scope,

101
00:08:50,068 --> 00:08:54,567
shared by the whole team, and it
can be copied for another campus.

102
00:08:55,407 --> 00:08:59,510
A scope can be exported
as text, a CSV table or

103
00:08:59,550 --> 00:09:03,575
a JSON file, or read
directly through the API.

104
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:08,475
On the comparison page, two
scopes sit side by side —

105
00:09:08,515 --> 00:09:13,519
even from different academic years —
and only the differences are shown.

106
00:09:14,667 --> 00:09:18,283
Each selected book can have
a note, a person responsible

107
00:09:18,323 --> 00:09:21,291
for following it up, a
status and a due date.

108
00:09:22,131 --> 00:09:25,119
Mona assigns tasks to her team in one go,

109
00:09:25,159 --> 00:09:28,726
and each member is notified
in their own language.

110
00:09:29,867 --> 00:09:33,306
On “My assignments”, each
member sees what they owe

111
00:09:33,346 --> 00:09:36,480
across all their
organizations, overdue first.

112
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:42,191
Reminders go out as a due date
approaches and once it has passed,

113
00:09:42,231 --> 00:09:48,555
and each member chooses to get assignment
emails right away or in one morning summary.

114
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:54,477
With each new academic year, the
platform team publishes an updated

115
00:09:54,517 --> 00:09:58,767
edition of the catalogue, and
schools are notified at once.

116
00:09:59,607 --> 00:10:03,160
In one step, the scope is
carried to the new year,

117
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:08,487
with a review showing what changed, what
was removed and what can be carried.

118
00:10:09,327 --> 00:10:14,407
Notes and responsibilities move with it,
so the team does not start from zero.

119
00:10:15,833 --> 00:10:19,859
Now to the classroom. The
school creates its students’

120
00:10:19,899 --> 00:10:23,654
accounts itself, one by
one or from a roster file.

121
00:10:24,494 --> 00:10:28,584
A preview catches the file’s
mistakes before import,

122
00:10:28,624 --> 00:10:33,426
with clear Arabic messages, then
gives out one-time passwords.

123
00:10:34,266 --> 00:10:38,303
Students need no email address
and cannot sign themselves

124
00:10:38,343 --> 00:10:41,668
up: the school is
responsible for the account.

125
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:46,891
Then the class is created: its name,
its grade and its curriculum from the

126
00:10:46,931 --> 00:10:50,660
school’s scope — then its students,
and a teacher for each subject.

127
00:11:01,833 --> 00:11:06,258
The teacher sets homework linked
to the curriculum’s books,

128
00:11:06,298 --> 00:11:09,829
with instructions, a due
time and a full mark.

129
00:11:10,669 --> 00:11:14,328
They see who handed in, who
was late and who has not,

130
00:11:14,368 --> 00:11:18,780
then mark it, write a comment and
return the work to the student.

131
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:24,414
Every file a student uploads is
scanned for viruses before anyone opens

132
00:11:24,454 --> 00:11:28,558
it, and photos are resized with
their location data removed.

133
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:33,409
Online tests come with complete tools:

134
00:11:33,449 --> 00:11:39,715
multiple choice, true or false,
short answers and written questions.

135
00:11:40,555 --> 00:11:45,230
The teacher sets the time, the number
of attempts and a start code read

136
00:11:45,270 --> 00:11:49,884
out in the room. The server keeps
the time — not the student’s device.

137
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:56,116
On the live monitoring screen,
the teacher sees each student’s

138
00:11:56,156 --> 00:11:59,432
state, the time left and
the number of answers.

139
00:12:00,272 --> 00:12:04,834
And sees what the page noticed:
leaving it, pasting into it,

140
00:12:04,874 --> 00:12:10,900
or losing the connection — with no camera,
no microphone and no screen recording.

141
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:17,358
They can give extra time or another
attempt, or close the test at once.

142
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:22,316
Every question is marked
automatically with fixed rules —

143
00:12:22,356 --> 00:12:27,886
accepting Arabic spelling variants — except
written answers, which the teacher marks.

144
00:12:28,726 --> 00:12:33,959
No AI sets any mark: the decision
always rests with the teacher.

145
00:12:35,100 --> 00:12:39,589
Marks come together in the
gradebook: a row per student,

146
00:12:39,629 --> 00:12:44,995
a column per homework and test,
with the average and a CSV download.

147
00:12:46,333 --> 00:12:50,927
So what does the teacher
gain? Their class’s homework,

148
00:12:50,967 --> 00:12:56,334
tests and marks in one place,
tied to the curriculum they teach.

149
00:12:57,174 --> 00:13:01,549
Manual follow-up gets lighter:
hand-ins and late work show up by

150
00:13:01,589 --> 00:13:05,774
themselves, and objective
questions are marked automatically.

151
00:13:06,614 --> 00:13:09,804
And the educational decision
stays in their hands:

152
00:13:09,844 --> 00:13:14,995
they review, adjust, write their comments,
and decide when students see the results.

153
00:13:16,533 --> 00:13:21,212
Around all of this are tools
for integration and governance:

154
00:13:21,252 --> 00:13:25,287
API keys, and webhooks with
their delivery history;

155
00:13:25,777 --> 00:13:30,109
retention periods for classroom
data that each school sets,

156
00:13:30,149 --> 00:13:36,297
with notice fourteen days before anything
is deleted — and an audit log for all of it.

157
00:13:41,433 --> 00:13:43,764
Now, let’s look from the student’s side.

158
00:13:44,604 --> 00:13:47,535
Omar signs in with his school code,

159
00:13:47,575 --> 00:13:51,593
username and password,
from a computer or a phone.

160
00:13:52,533 --> 00:13:56,928
The Student Portal opens on
“Today”: what he has to do,

161
00:13:56,968 --> 00:14:02,308
what he missed, what came back
marked, and the tests open right now.

162
00:14:03,433 --> 00:14:06,736
He opens the homework, writes
his answer or photographs

163
00:14:06,776 --> 00:14:09,475
his sheet and attaches
it, then hands it in.

164
00:14:10,315 --> 00:14:13,941
He can take his work back to
change it until it is marked,

165
00:14:13,981 --> 00:14:18,064
and when the teacher returns it,
he sees his mark and the comment.

166
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:21,873
In a test, he enters the start code,

167
00:14:21,913 --> 00:14:26,395
the countdown begins, and every
answer is saved as he writes it.

168
00:14:27,235 --> 00:14:30,583
If the connection weakens,
the page retries by

169
00:14:30,623 --> 00:14:33,775
itself and tells him
what is not saved yet.

170
00:14:34,615 --> 00:14:40,305
And the page tells him plainly what
the teacher can see during the test.

171
00:14:41,433 --> 00:14:45,320
After he submits, the result
appears when the teacher decides,

172
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:49,363
with the correct answers if the
teacher allows them to be shown.

173
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:53,898
In “My grades” Omar sees
his marks in each subject,

174
00:14:53,938 --> 00:14:57,699
and in “My books” his curriculum,
as his school chose it.

175
00:14:58,539 --> 00:15:03,868
A clear portal, in Arabic or
English, designed to be accessible to

176
00:15:03,908 --> 00:15:08,865
all, bringing the student’s
school day together in one place.

177
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:14,955
And what does the student gain?
One place for the school day,

178
00:15:14,995 --> 00:15:20,509
showing what is due and when, without
searching through scattered messages.

179
00:15:21,349 --> 00:15:25,399
And a fair test experience:
time kept by the server,

180
00:15:25,439 --> 00:15:32,027
equally for everyone, answers saved as they
go, and clarity about what the teacher sees.

181
00:15:32,867 --> 00:15:37,806
All in their own language, from their
phone, with an account their school manages.

182
00:15:39,633 --> 00:15:44,148
And what about artificial intelligence?
We want to be precise here.

183
00:15:44,988 --> 00:15:48,958
Today the platform includes no
artificial-intelligence feature,

184
00:15:48,998 --> 00:15:54,421
and its integration with the Wise EduAgent
educational assistant has not been built yet.

185
00:15:55,261 --> 00:15:58,567
Inspecting the Wise
application and choosing

186
00:15:58,607 --> 00:16:01,778
an AI provider are both steps still ahead.

187
00:16:03,433 --> 00:16:08,553
What we show now is a future
vision, not a feature ready to sell.

188
00:16:09,393 --> 00:16:12,960
An educational assistant
inside the same student portal,

189
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:17,440
with the same account, that knows
the curriculum the school has chosen.

190
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:21,460
It could help a student
understand a lesson,

191
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:26,419
organize their study and practise
questions, and help teachers prepare.

192
00:16:27,259 --> 00:16:32,476
Its rules are set in advance: every
answer is labelled as machine-generated,

193
00:16:32,516 --> 00:16:35,874
it never changes an
official record or a grade,

194
00:16:35,914 --> 00:16:40,298
and it is subject to usage limits
within the same subscription.

195
00:16:41,138 --> 00:16:46,876
AI here is meant to support teachers and
students — not to replace the teacher.

196
00:16:48,300 --> 00:16:52,713
The foundation it needs is
ready today: student identity,

197
00:16:52,753 --> 00:16:57,944
curriculum context, subscriptions,
usage metering and the audit log.

198
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:07,632
The platform runs on an
institutional subscription model:

199
00:17:07,672 --> 00:17:13,044
a free plan, paid plans, and an
enterprise plan under a custom contract.

200
00:17:13,884 --> 00:17:18,546
The prices shown are launch
proposals, open to review.

201
00:17:19,386 --> 00:17:23,539
Each plan’s limits are
enforced on the server:

202
00:17:23,579 --> 00:17:29,346
members, scopes, exports, API
requests and the features available.

203
00:17:30,186 --> 00:17:33,781
The system runs the
whole subscription cycle:

204
00:17:33,821 --> 00:17:39,697
trial, upgrade, renewal, invoices, and
a grace period when a payment is late.

205
00:17:40,537 --> 00:17:45,739
The Paymob payment integration is
built and tested against a simulation

206
00:17:45,779 --> 00:17:50,641
of its interface, and awaits the
merchant account keys to go live.

207
00:17:51,967 --> 00:17:56,173
Technically, all organizations
run on one system,

208
00:17:56,213 --> 00:18:02,272
but each one’s data is isolated by
security policies inside the database.

209
00:18:03,112 --> 00:18:05,777
So even if a piece of code were wrong,

210
00:18:05,817 --> 00:18:10,388
the database would refuse to show
one organization’s data to another.

211
00:18:11,228 --> 00:18:16,131
Stored secrets are encrypted, with
tools to rotate the encryption

212
00:18:16,171 --> 00:18:20,169
keys, and request limits
that protect against abuse.

213
00:18:21,700 --> 00:18:26,120
The platform is containerized, with
two application instances behind

214
00:18:26,160 --> 00:18:30,520
a load balancer, so it can be
updated without stopping the service.

215
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:36,133
In a load test on a local environment,
three hundred students took one

216
00:18:36,173 --> 00:18:40,756
test from a single network, without
a single request being refused.

217
00:18:41,596 --> 00:18:46,693
Encrypted backups are taken, and
restoring them has been rehearsed

218
00:18:46,733 --> 00:18:51,330
— including rebuilding a lost
server from an off-site copy.

219
00:18:52,867 --> 00:18:57,465
And because it is SaaS, every update
to the catalogue or the system

220
00:18:57,505 --> 00:19:01,659
reaches every institution
together, with nothing to install.

221
00:19:02,499 --> 00:19:07,304
When AI is added, it can be
offered as an add-on module within

222
00:19:07,344 --> 00:19:11,864
the existing subscriptions,
with no second billing system.

223
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:18,453
And what does the institution gain?
A documented curriculum decision the

224
00:19:18,493 --> 00:19:22,844
